Subject: [PATCH] Runtime: a host layer can keep a state from being saved (a native function in the middle of a call)

A state holds the guest machine, not the host's own stacks. A native host layer can run functions natively on stacks of
their own, and a native function that waits in the middle of a call (the pause menu, in a level) has native frames no
state can carry: a state saved then fails when loaded (the bridge's sentinel finds nothing to resume - TPepperoni, private
PR #16). setSaveStateBlockerHook lets such a layer say why a state cannot be taken right now; saveStateBlocker asks it
after the scheduler's own reasons, so the save waits and retries as it does for those (600 ticks).

--- a/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/ee_scheduler.h
+++ b/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/ee_scheduler.h
@@ -587,6 +587,9 @@
     // What keeps a state from being taken right now (host callbacks that cannot be
     // written to a file), or empty.
     [[nodiscard]] std::string saveStateBlocker() const;
+    // A host layer's own reason a state cannot be taken right now (empty: none), asked by saveStateBlocker after the
+    // scheduler's reasons - a host layer's native frames in the middle of a call. On the EE executor.
+    void setSaveStateBlockerHook(std::function<std::string()> hook) { m_saveStateBlockerHook = std::move(hook); }
     void saveState(ps2x::StateWriter &out) const;
     void loadState(ps2x::StateReader &in);

@@ -828,5 +831,6 @@
     mutable std::mutex m_refusedStateMutex;
     std::string m_lastRefusedState;
     std::function<void()> m_stateLoadedHook; // setStateLoadedHook
+    std::function<std::string()> m_saveStateBlockerHook; // setSaveStateBlockerHook
     uint64_t m_saveStateGiveUpTick = 0;
 };
--- a/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/EeScheduler.cpp
+++ b/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/EeScheduler.cpp
@@ -2784,5 +2784,7 @@
         if (invocation.onComplete)
             return "a guest call for the host is queued";
     }
+    if (m_saveStateBlockerHook)
+        return m_saveStateBlockerHook();
     return {};
 }
